# Normalise line endings across platforms.
# Every text file is committed with LF; binaries are left untouched.

* text=auto eol=lf

# F# sources: explicit LF — never CRLF even on Windows checkouts.
*.fs        text eol=lf
*.fsi       text eol=lf
*.fsx       text eol=lf
*.fsproj    text eol=lf

# C# + related.
*.cs        text eol=lf
*.csproj    text eol=lf
*.sln       text eol=lf
*.props     text eol=lf
*.targets   text eol=lf

# Prose / config.
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf

# Binary markers — prevent accidental diff / line-ending munging.
*.pdf       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.zip       binary
*.tar.gz    binary
*.tgz       binary
*.nupkg     binary
*.snupkg    binary
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.pdb       binary
*.jar       binary

# Diagnostic artifacts (corruption triage, profile dumps, etc.):
# track but suppress diffs so PR review stays focused on
# conclusions, not raw evidence walls. The artifacts directory
# is mostly text (fsck output / rev-list output / config
# snapshots) — text compresses well in git pack-delta storage,
# so the cost is review-readability, not soul-storage. Per the
# recalibrated soulfile-cleanliness rule
# (memory/feedback_repo_is_soulfile_dont_commit_raw_diagnostic_dumps_*.md):
# - text artifacts → track freely; .gitattributes diff
#   suppression for review-noise
# - binary artifacts → git-lfs or non-soul repo
# Note: `-merge` is intentionally NOT used here. `-merge` unsets
# the merge driver (binary-file behavior) and would break 3-way
# text merges with conflict markers — undesirable for the
# tracked text artifacts. `-diff` alone gives the diff
# suppression we want without changing merge semantics.
# Narrow to raw-evidence file types only — fsck/rev-list outputs,
# refs dumps, config snapshots, log files. Markdown summaries (like
# triage-report.md) are NOT suppressed — those are human-readable
# conclusions and should remain reviewable in PRs.
docs/lost-substrate/artifacts/**/*.txt linguist-generated=true -diff
docs/lost-substrate/artifacts/**/*.log linguist-generated=true -diff

# memory/MEMORY.md is an unordered append-only index. Multiple
# in-flight PRs each adding a row produced recurring rebase conflicts
# (always trivial — keep both entries — but a token-tax recurring
# friction). The `union` merge driver auto-appends both sides on
# conflict, eliminating manual resolution. Multi-AI flagged: Claude.ai,
# Gemini, Ani, Deepseek (4 reviewers in the 2026-04-30 session), Gemini
# named the mechanism. Composes with Otto-363 substrate-or-it-didn't-
# happen — index-row landings are substrate; the merge driver makes
# their landing structurally robust.
memory/MEMORY.md merge=union

# Project Genesis prototype — the served files under genesis/ (except the
# _src/ snapshot) are a Vite production build: minified JS/CSS bundles, not
# authored source. Mark them generated so linguist excludes them from language
# stats and `-diff` keeps the 250KB minified bundle out of PR review walls.
# The reproducible source lives in genesis/_src/ and stays reviewable.
genesis/assets/**   linguist-generated=true -diff
genesis/index.html  linguist-generated=true

# Heartbeat lane merge semantics (2026-08-17).
# `prepare-heartbeat-branch.ts` rebuilds each `heartbeat/<agent>` lane over current main and
# squash-carries the previous lane's unflushed delta. A flush snapshots the lane, but the lane
# keeps ticking while the flush PR is in flight, so main routinely lands a strict PREFIX of that
# delta. The next tick's merge base then predates the flush and both sides insert at the same
# end-of-file anchor -- a vacuous conflict (main's side of the hunk measured EMPTY on the live
# lanes) that wedged alexa, otto and soraya in rotation for hours on 2026-08-16.
#
# All three paths below are SINGLE-WRITER: the two are agent-scoped by filename, and
# data/vault-state.json is generated by otto's lane alone (agent-heartbeat.yml: "only otto
# generates vault-state"). Main's copy is never a rival author's work, only an older snapshot of
# the same lane's output -- which is what makes an automatic resolution honest, not a clobber.
#
# Append-only log -> `union`, which is exactly set-union here: verified lossless in both
# directions with zero duplicate lines against the live alexa and otto lanes.
db/mutation-findings/*.jsonl            merge=union
#
# `docs/github/prs/manifest.jsonl` — added 2026-08-17 after it wedged soraya's tick at 02:43Z
# (run 31988867656). The 2026-08-16 fix worked exactly as designed on that run: the declared
# `db/mutation-findings/soraya.jsonl` reported `Auto-merging`, and the tick still failed because a
# FOURTH append-only file, not in the declared set, conflicted. That is the preparer's typed
# backpressure doing its job -- an undeclared conflicting path must fail the tick -- so this is a
# new member of the class, not a regression of the fix.
#
# The justification here is NOT single-writer, and saying so matters: two workflows append to it
# (`pr-archive-on-merge.yml`, `agent-heartbeat.yml`) plus the derive/migrate tools. It is safe for a
# different and STRONGER reason -- append-only with a globally unique key, and that key's uniqueness
# is ENFORCED rather than assumed: `pr-manifest-integrity.yml` fails on a "duplicate-keyed shard".
# So if `union` ever did duplicate a row, an existing check goes red. Measured on this tip:
# 6776 lines, 6776 distinct `pr_number` -- one row per PR, exactly the shape set-union preserves.
#
# The failure mode this does NOT cover, stated: union is wrong for an EDIT to an existing row (it
# would keep both versions). Nothing edits rows today -- archival appends once per PR on merge --
# and `pr-manifest-integrity` would catch the duplicate if that ever changed.
docs/github/prs/manifest.jsonl           merge=union
#
# `data/rs-blocks.jsonl` — added 2026-08-17 after it wedged otto's and soraya's ticks at 16:43Z
# (run 32046921903) with `CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in data/rs-blocks.jsonl`. Fifth
# member of the same class, surfaced the same way: the preparer's typed backpressure refused an
# undeclared conflicting path, which is the design working, not a regression. It surfaced now
# because the flush outage let the lanes run many ticks ahead of main, widening exactly the
# partial-flush window this block exists for.
#
# Measured on this tip, not assumed:
#   - append-only — the last 12 commits touching it are each `+1 -0`, no line ever modified
#   - 13 rows, 13 distinct `(agent, seq)` — the key is per-agent-sequential
#   - written by otto, alexa and soraya, so this is NOT single-writer by file; it is
#     single-writer PER ROW: the `agent` field names the lane that emitted it, and a lane only
#     ever appends its own rows. Main's copy therefore holds this lane's already-flushed rows
#     plus other lanes' rows, never a rival edit to the same row.
#
# The partial-flush case is where a naive reading fears duplication: after a flush, main and the
# lane both contain the same row. Git resolves an identical addition on both sides as ONE change
# and emits a single copy — the same mechanism already verified in production for the four paths
# above. Union duplicates only when the two sides add DIFFERENT lines, which is the case we want.
#
# STATED LIMIT, because the manifest entry above gets to make a stronger claim and this one does
# not: `(agent, seq)` uniqueness is NOT enforced by any check. `verify-rs-blocks.ts` does run in
# this workflow, but it verifies each block's Reed-Solomon syndrome — corruption of a row's
# contents — which is a different property and would not notice a duplicated row. Union is safe
# here on the append-only + per-row-single-writer argument alone; if rows ever become editable,
# this line must go.
data/rs-blocks.jsonl                     merge=union
#
# `data/ci-runs.jsonl` — added 2026-08-22 after it wedged otto's, alexa's and soraya's ticks at
# 22:05Z and 22:24Z (runs on 79ff032a2 / 9f861ebef) with
# `CONFLICT (add/add): Merge conflict in data/ci-runs.jsonl`, plus the alexa flush job. SEVENTH
# member of the class and the drift-rate sibling of `rs-blocks.jsonl` directly above.
#
# WHY IT APPEARED NOW AND NOT BEFORE. The path is four hours old. Until #13928 the heartbeat step
# ran `git add data/ci-runs.jsonl` and never committed, so every tick appended to a working-tree
# file that was discarded with the runner — the workflow says so itself: "has never existed on
# main ... green on every tick and produced nothing". The moment that step started COMMITTING, all
# three lanes began creating the same brand-new path independently, which is ADD/ADD by
# construction: the merge base has neither side.
#
# Measured on this tip, not assumed:
#   - append-only — the sole writer is `appendFileSync(dataPath, ...)` in
#     `observe/drift-rate-cli.ts` `doRecord`; no code path rewrites an existing line
#   - every row carries a `lane` field naming the emitting agent, so this is single-writer
#     PER ROW exactly as `rs-blocks.jsonl` is, not single-writer per file
#   - main's copy therefore holds this lane's flushed rows plus other lanes' rows, never a
#     rival edit to the same row
#
# REPRODUCED AND VERIFIED in a scratch repo rather than argued:
#   - two branches each CREATING the file -> `CONFLICT (add/add)`, the live message verbatim
#   - the same pair with this line present -> "Auto-merging", clean, both lanes' rows kept,
#     zero conflict markers
#   - the partial-flush case (both sides carry the already-flushed rows, the lane has one more)
#     -> clean, and `sort | uniq -d` is EMPTY: identical additions on both sides resolve to one
#     copy, so union does not duplicate a flushed row
#
# STATED LIMIT, same as the sibling above: no check enforces row uniqueness here. Union is safe on
# the append-only + per-row-single-writer argument alone. If a row ever becomes editable — a
# backfill that rewrites an outcome, say — this line must go.
data/ci-runs.jsonl                       merge=union
#
# `data/tick-reasoning.jsonl` — added 2026-08-26 after it wedged alexa's tick from 23:42Z onward
# (run 33024333706) with `CONFLICT (add/add): Merge conflict in data/tick-reasoning.jsonl`.
# EIGHTH member of the class, and the first that wedged exactly ONE lane while its two siblings
# ticked green on the same run — which is what localises the cause to the merge BASE rather than
# to anything shared.
#
# WHY ONLY ALEXA. The path reached main for the first time at 23:11:11Z, in the alexa flush
# `18e00976d2` (#15551). Measured on that tip:
#     lane      merge-base   base has file   lane has file   main has file
#     alexa     443cdacdb5   NO              YES             YES   -> ADD/ADD, wedged
#     otto      64005b76ac   YES             YES             YES   -> 3-way content merge, green
#     soraya    64005b76ac   YES             YES             YES   -> 3-way content merge, green
# otto and soraya were rebuilt over a main that already carried the file, so their base has it and
# git has a common ancestor to diff against. alexa's base predates its own flush, so the base has
# neither side. This is SELF-SUSTAINING: the failed tick never advances the lane, so the base never
# moves past the flush, so the next tick reproduces the same add/add. It cannot clear on its own.
#
# Measured on this tip, not assumed:
#   - append-only — the sole writer is `appendFileSync(path, JSON.stringify(reasoning) + "\n")` in
#     `observe/tick-reasoning.ts` `recordReasoning`; no code path rewrites an existing line
#   - every row carries an `agent` field naming the emitting lane, so this is single-writer PER ROW
#     exactly as `ci-runs.jsonl` and `rs-blocks.jsonl` are
#   - 16 rows on main, 30 on the lane, all 16 parse; main's rows are a strict SUBSET of the lane's
#     (`comm -23` = 0 rows only-main, 14 rows only-lane). Main's side of the hunk is EMPTY, the
#     same vacuous-conflict signature this whole block exists for.
#
# REPRODUCED against the live refs rather than argued: `checkout -B lane origin/main` then
# `merge --squash origin/heartbeat/alexa` emits the failing message VERBATIM without this line,
# and "Auto-merging data/tick-reasoning.jsonl" with it — 30 rows, zero duplicates, zero markers.
#
# STATED LIMITS, three of them.
#   1. No check enforces row uniqueness here, as with both siblings above. Union is safe on the
#      append-only + per-row-single-writer argument alone. If a row ever becomes editable, this
#      line must go.
#   2. `recordReasoning` swallows its own write error (`catch { /* non-fatal */ }`), so a row can
#      go MISSING without anything going red. That is a gap in the writer, not something union
#      makes worse, and it is not fixed here.
#   3. UNION CAN DUPLICATE A FLUSHED ROW, and this was measured rather than assumed while adding
#      this line. Where the two sides share rows and main additionally holds a FOREIGN row placed
#      BEFORE the shared ones, xdiff cannot align the common prefix and emits both copies of the
#      shared row — never data loss, but one row counted twice. Measured over three orderings:
#          main=[A1]      lane=[A1,A2]   -> 2 rows, 0 dupes   (the LIVE shape today)
#          main=[A1,O]    lane=[A1,A2]   -> 3 rows, 0 dupes   (realistic post-flush; pinned by test)
#          main=[O,A1]    lane=[A1,A2]   -> 4 rows, 1 DUPE
#      This is a property of the `union` driver, so it applies equally to the four union paths
#      declared above; it is recorded here because this is where it was first measured. It does
#      not block this line: the live file is single-lane and main is a strict prefix of the lane
#      (16 of 30 rows, `comm -23` = 0), which is the clean case. `manifest.jsonl` is the only
#      union path with a duplicate-key check behind it; the rest, including this one, are not
#      covered, and a duplicate here would inflate the decorrelation meter's denominator.
data/tick-reasoning.jsonl                merge=union
#
# Regenerated-per-tick snapshots -> `theirs` (the lane's). `union` is WRONG for these and was
# measured producing two concatenated objects, i.e. invalid JSON: .rs-buffer-*.json is a
# single-line rolling buffer and vault-state/roster are whole-file rollups. The tick rewrites
# them immediately afterwards, so the carried value is transient either way.
#
# NOTE: `theirs` is not a built-in driver; `prepare-heartbeat-branch.ts` registers it in local
# repo config. A clone that never calls the preparer silently ignores these three lines and keeps
# stock conflict behaviour, so this cannot change merge semantics for ordinary repository work.
docs/observe-events/.rs-buffer-*.json   merge=theirs
data/vault-state.json                   merge=theirs
data/vault-roster.json                  merge=theirs
#
# `docs/github/prs/shards/**/*.json` — added 2026-08-18 after soraya's tick wedged on a run of
# `CONFLICT (add/add)` across many shards at once. SIXTH member of the class, and the first that
# is ADD/ADD rather than a content conflict: two branches each CREATED the same shard, so the
# merge base has neither side.
#
# WHY TAKING ONE SIDE IS LOSSLESS HERE, and this is the repo's own rule rather than one invented
# for the merge driver. Measured on the wedging pair (PR 9180, shard .../009/…23dc.json): both
# files are 545 bytes and differ in EXACTLY TWO keys —
#     fetched_at:  main 2026-07-02T18:35:46.863Z   lane 2026-08-17T13:47:37.443Z
#     commit_sha:  main 764d57a2…                  lane 8ca0ad39…
# `pr_number`, `archive_path`, `title`, `source_ids` and `schema_version` are byte-identical.
# Those are precisely the two fields `pr-manifest-shards.ts` already excludes from equality, in
# `equalModuloWallClockNoise`: "the two fields that drift between runs WITHOUT indicating any
# substrate change — `fetched_at` (wall clock) and `commit_sha` (moves whenever main moves)."
# The writer honours that rule and preserves existing bytes on a match. So the codebase ALREADY
# declares these two records the same record; picking either is picking between two spellings of
# one fact.
#
# WHY THE WRITER'S OWN GUARD COULD NOT PREVENT THIS, stated so nobody reads this as a writer bug:
# `writeShard` can only preserve bytes it can SEE — its check is `existsSync(path)` in ONE
# checkout. Two branches that each create the shard from a tree where it is absent never observe
# each other, so the guard cannot engage. That is inherent to concurrent creation on divergent
# branches, not a defect in `writeShard`, and it is why the resolution belongs at the merge layer.
#
# THE FAILURE MODE THIS DOES NOT COVER, stated because it is the real risk: if two sides ever
# differ SUBSTANTIVELY — a different `archive_path` or `title` for the same `pr_number` — `theirs`
# takes the lane's and drops main's silently. Nothing here detects that. It is not reachable by
# the wall-clock drift above, and `pr-manifest-integrity` catches duplicate keys rather than
# divergent values, so if shards ever become editable in place this line must be revisited.
docs/github/prs/shards/**/*.json        merge=theirs
#
# `docs/history/pr-reviews/PR-####-*.md` — added 2026-08-18. SEVENTH member of the class, and
# the one deliberately left undeclared until now. It is recorded at length because the reason
# for the earlier delay was WRONG, and the correction is the whole justification for the line.
#
# THE EARLIER, INCORRECT REASON. This path was parked with the note that `theirs` "would
# silently drop a recorded review thread." That inverted the merge direction.
# `prepare-heartbeat-branch.ts` does `checkout -B <lane> origin/main` and then
# `merge --squash <previous lane>` — so OURS is main and THEIRS is the LANE. `merge=theirs`
# keeps the lane's copy, and the lane is the side that HAS the threads. Main's copy is the
# impoverished one, written when the review threads were not fetched (or the fetch came back
# empty). The rule was being applied backwards.
#
# MEASURED on origin/main vs origin/heartbeat/soraya, whole-path, not sampled:
#   git diff --name-status origin/main FETCH_HEAD -- docs/history/pr-reviews/
#     1206  D  — present on main, ABSENT on the lane. Not conflicts; a merge never touches them.
#        2  M  — the ONLY files present on BOTH sides with differing content.
# Those two are exactly the pair the failing tick names (run 32104099738):
#     PR-9181  lane 6247 bytes / 1 `PRRT_` thread   main 2847 bytes / 0 threads
#     PR-9182  lane 8153 bytes / 2 `PRRT_` threads  main 3474 bytes / 0 threads
# In 2 of 2 live cases the lane's copy strictly dominates: it contains main's record plus the
# threads. So `theirs` PRESERVES the review threads here; it is main's copy that loses data.
# The converse case this was parked over — main richer, `theirs` dropping it — has ZERO
# instances in the live data.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE LANE, since a wedged lane alone was tolerated for hours: the tick
# that cannot land is soraya's, and `pr-manifest-integrity.yml` names soraya's archive duty as
# the repair writer for the derived PR index. With the lane wedged the index drifts past its
# ~1h repair bound and main goes red (run 32107528665, 06:34Z). Re-deriving the index (#11809)
# clears the symptom for about an hour; only this unwedges the writer.
#
# STATED LIMITS, both of them, because this line rests on a smaller sample than the others:
#   1. N = 2. The direction is consistent and it is the whole both-present population today,
#      but it is two files, not a proof about the generator.
#   2. `theirs` is right only while main's copy is the stale one. If the archival producer is
#      ever fixed so main holds the richer record and a lane can write a thread-less copy over
#      it, this line inverts from lossless to lossy. The durable fix is producer-side — archive
#      with the threads fetched — and this line should be REMOVED when that lands, not kept as
#      belt-and-braces.
docs/history/pr-reviews/PR-*.md         merge=theirs

# Git-native inverted index (081M0QTXTR3087G0R002R439FH) — derived, machine-written,
# regenerate-never-merge. Marked generated so it collapses in review by default:
# the artifact IS reviewable text (that is why it is JSONL), but 40 shard files of
# postings should not drown the code changes in a PR that touches both.
# On conflict: rebuild, never hand-merge.
db/search-index/inverted/*.jsonl   linguist-generated=true
db/search-index/inverted/files.txt linguist-generated=true
